1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:06,834
If people from Gefersa to Dejazmach
Yigezu's quarter asked who the famous

2
00:00:06,834 --> 00:00:14,287
chicken trader of Gulele was, they were
told: Tebeje. Tebeje the drunk. Tebeje the

3
00:00:14,287 --> 00:00:15,650
chicken trader.

4
00:00:16,158 --> 00:00:23,477
Whether he knew them or not, at night he
greeted everyone. Whether he knew them or

5
00:00:23,477 --> 00:00:26,958
not, at night he laughed with everyone.

6
00:00:27,450 --> 00:00:35,652
When coffee was brewed by every hearth in
Gulele, every drunkard's tale anyone told

7
00:00:35,652 --> 00:00:43,953
was told about Tebeje. Even old widows who
had never seen his face sipped from their

8
00:00:43,953 --> 00:00:48,400
cups and pinned other drunks' stories on
him.

9
00:00:48,908 --> 00:00:59,025
This was true. Whether worse than they
said or better, he came out of Gulele's

10
00:00:59,025 --> 00:01:06,808
eucalyptus and appeared to Addis Ababa as
a strange new man.

11
00:01:07,325 --> 00:01:17,064
By the old timber customs, heading east,
across the bridge over the river, the

12
00:01:17,064 --> 00:01:27,178
first small thatched hut you reach: that
is Tebeje's house. There, in that house,

13
00:01:27,178 --> 00:01:29,675
in Gulele, he lived.

14
00:01:30,158 --> 00:01:37,649
He carried the timber and dug the earth;
he built the house himself and paid for

15
00:01:37,649 --> 00:01:40,458
the thatch. Tebeje felt proud.

16
00:01:40,950 --> 00:01:49,150
Then his eyes filled with tears. A tear
ran down his cheek, and he crushed it with

17
00:01:49,150 --> 00:01:51,750
the palm of his left hand.

18
00:01:52,242 --> 00:02:01,421
'Which chicken trader?' 'That drunkard of
Gulele.' 'Oh! Me, Tebeje, I am ruined,' he

19
00:02:01,421 --> 00:02:02,842
said, shaken.

20
00:02:03,325 --> 00:02:09,925
'From now on, for the honour of this house
at least, I must quit drinking,' he said.

21
00:02:10,408 --> 00:02:16,131
'Gulele is asleep. If I sit under this
eucalyptus and drink, no one will see me,'

22
00:02:16,131 --> 00:02:16,908
he thought.

23
00:02:17,408 --> 00:02:21,608
'I've slurped about half of it,' he said,
and burst out laughing.

24
00:02:22,117 --> 00:02:28,859
'Where did you last see the maid?' he
asked, and began diving and swimming to

25
00:02:28,859 --> 00:02:29,647
find her.

26
00:02:30,158 --> 00:02:36,605
'Is the woman saved?' he asked tenderly.
One came closer: 'It's a pig you pulled

27
00:02:36,605 --> 00:02:37,008
out.'

28
00:02:37,492 --> 00:02:45,810
All his good, brave deeds were counted
cheap, because he was known to be always

29
00:02:45,810 --> 00:02:46,442
drunk.

30
00:02:46,950 --> 00:02:54,600
From five months after he built the house,
Tebeje never drank at all.

31
00:02:55,117 --> 00:03:02,988
'If I slip out, just today, just a little,
just once, just me... what harm could it

32
00:03:02,988 --> 00:03:03,367
do?'

33
00:03:03,867 --> 00:03:08,970
Like a beloved leader they carried him on
their shoulders, passing him along,

34
00:03:08,970 --> 00:03:09,567
cheering.

35
00:03:10,075 --> 00:03:20,368
A great force struck him from behind. A
fox hole caught him in front. His leg

36
00:03:20,368 --> 00:03:23,175
shattered like glass.

37
00:03:23,658 --> 00:03:31,563
But another said, 'So he never drinks
again, I'll cut it off at the hip,' and he

38
00:03:31,563 --> 00:03:31,958
did.

39
00:03:32,450 --> 00:03:40,550
Just then the dogs fighting over a bone
tumbled onto him, and he woke in terror.

40
00:03:41,033 --> 00:03:47,644
'How many legs do I have?' he asked.
Weyzero Tiruneshe replied, 'How many did

41
00:03:47,644 --> 00:03:49,533
you have before, sir?'

42
00:03:50,033 --> 00:03:58,689
'Truly, you have two legs, sir,' she said.
Tebeje bent to kiss his leg, but it was

43
00:03:58,689 --> 00:03:59,533
too far.

44
00:04:00,033 --> 00:04:09,629
Looking at the sky he said, 'For me, drink
means death.' It was night. 'Nights of

45
00:04:09,629 --> 00:04:14,723
such vows pass,' Weyzero Tiruneshe
replied.

